{"parentUuid":"6a6dbcd9-dc70-4a36-b851-4ad47c8b4103","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y5t8DZUgfCuLnGqiyVfGKv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Intel VTune Windows free 2025 profile C++ Python download\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Fix Performance Bottlenecks with Intel® VTune™ Profiler\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/tools/oneapi/vtune-profiler.html\"},{\"title\":\"Get Started with Intel® VTune™ Profiler for Windows* OS\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/vtune-profiler/get-started-guide/2025-4/windows-os.html\"},{\"title\":\"Get Intel® VTune™ Profiler\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/tools/oneapi/vtune-profiler-download.html\"},{\"title\":\"Profiling Artificial Intelligence Applications (NEW)\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/vtune-profiler/cookbook/2025-4/profiling-ai-applications.html\"},{\"title\":\"VTune - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VTune\"},{\"title\":\"Install on Windows* OS\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/vtune-profiler/installation-guide/2025-0/windows.html\"},{\"title\":\"Intel® oneAPI Base Toolkit Release Notes\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/release-notes/oneapi-base-toolkit/2025.html\"},{\"title\":\"Intel® VTune™ Profiler System Requirements\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/system-requirements/vtune-profiler/2025-1.html\"},{\"title\":\"Python* Code Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/vtune-profiler/user-guide/2025-4/python-code-analysis.html\"},{\"title\":\"Download Intel VTune Performance Analyzer\",\"url\":\"https://intel-vtune-performance-analyzer.apponic.com/download/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Intel VTune for Windows in 2025:\n\n## Free Availability\n\nVTune is available for free as a stand-alone tool or as part of the Intel oneAPI Base Toolkit.\n\n## Windows Installation\n\nYou can install Intel® VTune™ Profiler on a Windows system, either as a standalone application or as a component in the Intel® oneAPI Base Toolkit. You can download Intel® VTune™ Profiler for Windows OS as an online or offline installer.\n\n## Supported Languages\n\nVTune can profile SYCL*, C, C++, C#, Fortran, OpenCL™ code, Python*, Google Go* programming language, Java*, .NET, Assembly, or any combination of languages.\n\n## Python Profiling Features\n\nThe 2025 version adds profiling support for Python 3.11 with improved productivity through the ability to focus Python profiling to only areas of interest and control performance data collection with ITT APIs.\n\n## Download Options\n\nThe search results reference download pages but don't display specific download links. You can find the official download at Intel's VTune Profiler download page (intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/tools/oneapi/vtune-profiler-download.html) where you can choose between online and offline installers for Windows.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"762cb2be-d29a-489c-9952-d23984f5460e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:08:57.869Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6a6dbcd9-dc70-4a36-b851-4ad47c8b4103","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b043add5-20f2-4919-a4ab-0faff818f699","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XFFkG8BhRzmLvkUXGYFjrC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Linux perf record flamegraph C++ tutorial 2025 native python\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"[Linux] Profiling —visualize program bottleneck with Flamegraph | by TechHara | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@techhara/profiling-visualize-program-bottleneck-with-flamegraph-3e0c5855b2fe\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - brendangregg/FlameGraph: Stack trace visualizer · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/brendangregg/FlameGraph\"},{\"title\":\"CPU Flame Graphs\",\"url\":\"https://www.brendangregg.com/FlameGraphs/cpuflamegraphs.html\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 27. Getting started with flamegraphs | Monitoring and managing system status and performance | Red Hat Enterprise Linux | 8 | Red Hat Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/8/html/monitoring_and_managing_system_status_and_performance/getting-started-with-flamegraphs_monitoring-and-managing-system-status-and-performance\"},{\"title\":\"Linux perf Examples\",\"url\":\"https://www.brendangregg.com/perf.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - perf: Linux profiling with performance counters\",\"url\":\"https://perfwiki.github.io/main/tutorial/\"},{\"title\":\"Quick steps of how to create a flame graph using perf · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://gist.github.com/trevnorris/9616784\"},{\"title\":\"Flame Graphs\",\"url\":\"https://www.brendangregg.com/flamegraphs.html\"},{\"title\":\"Use perf and FlameGraph to profile program on Linux | Nan Xiao's Blog\",\"url\":\"https://nanxiao.me/en/use-perf-and-flamegraph-to-profile-program-on-linux/\"},{\"title\":\"7.2 perf + FlameGraph Tutorial | Bianbu | SpacemiT Developer\",\"url\":\"https://bianbu.spacemit.com/en/brdk/Advanced_development/7.2_perf/\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive search results on using Linux perf with flamegraphs for performance profiling. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Basic Workflow\n\nTo use perf with flamegraphs, you'll need to install the perf utility with commands like `sudo apt install linux-tools-common linux-tools-generic linux-tools-`uname -r``.\n\n## Native Code Profiling (C++)\n\nFor profiling native applications like C/C++ programs that are compiled ahead-of-time (AOT), perf is the appropriate tool, unlike Java/C#/Python programs which run on a virtual machine and need dedicated tools.\n\n## Flamegraph Generation Process\n\nThe flamegraph generation involves creating line-based output for flamegraph.pl to read, using simple Perl programs named stackcollapse*.pl to process the output from different profilers.\n\n## Modern Built-in Support\n\nThe perf tool now supports natively generating flame graphs using the `perf script report flamegraph` command, which simplifies the workflow compared to older manual methods.\n\n## Python Profiling Limitations\n\nPython programs run on a virtual machine, so they need dedicated tools for profiling rather than perf directly.\n\nThe search results contain practical tutorials and examples from authoritative sources like Brendan Gregg's FlameGraph repository and recent documentation updated in 2025.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"872958fd-5bc3-4daf-8488-b4bcb8bddbbc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:08:59.163Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b043add5-20f2-4919-a4ab-0faff818f699","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d8c5f460-d00d-480e-be4f-e0ea865c81f6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q9RdE3qtQxZ3AyfCUiiWJU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT log_search_progress subsolver statistics interpretation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cpsat-logutils · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpsat-logutils/\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-logutils: Utils for parsing the logs of OR-Tools' CP-SAT solver.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-logutils\"},{\"title\":\"v9.8.3296 How do you log intermediate progress? · google/or-tools · Discussion #3989\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3989\"},{\"title\":\"Speeding up the time to find a first solution\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/AI6oGqyhx5Q\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Custom combination of subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/pQ4B7QD6ebw\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver takes way too long to shut down or does not stop at all despite given time limit · Issue #4882 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4882\"},{\"title\":\"Performance regression in `main` after an optimal solution already found · Issue #4268 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4268\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about CP-SAT `log_search_progress` and subsolver statistics interpretation:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe subsolver information section in CP-SAT logs provides detailed information about the different subsolvers that are used, which is a very detailed part of the log and can be overwhelming. This information is primarily intended for the developers of CP-SAT.\n\n## What Subsolver Statistics Reveal\n\nThe subsolver information gives you insights into how the various subsolvers have been contributing to the solution, how the otherwise hidden LP-techniques, including cutting planes, have been used, and how the different heuristics have been applied.\n\n## Types of Subsolvers\n\nMultiple different subsolvers may be running, including: default_lp, fixed, pseudo_costs, no_lp, max_lp, feasibility_pump, rnd_var_lns_default, rnd_cst_lns_default, graph_var_lns_default, graph_cst_lns_default, scheduling_time_window_lns_default, scheduling_random_lns_default, rins_lns_default, and rens_lns_default.\n\n## Subsolver Statistics Parameter\n\nThe `log_subsolver_statistics` parameter prints subsolver specific info at the end of the solver run, as distinct from the continuous `log_search_progress` output.\n\n## Practical Use\n\nBased on this subsolver data, you could try to tune the various parameters of CP-SAT for your problem. However, since the log is event-driven and can be challenging to interpret, the Log Analyzer helps by automatically plotting these values in a more understandable way.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a12fe1c8-5f3c-4b8c-ad56-d9d53685e635","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:00.983Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d8c5f460-d00d-480e-be4f-e0ea865c81f6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
