{"parentUuid":"6c371fc7-9e31-4e7b-80f2-3de7f4720092","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Wyg3Rsmtcg7e6FfBSg9Ntw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XR2oDKx1A8f1FYNKC87Xv2","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"superluminal\" OR \"tracy\" profiler windows C++ sampling 2025"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3907,"cache_read_input_tokens":29381,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3907,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":46,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaotnA9JYn5X2csGzTmXk","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"18485255-a3ad-4cba-a3a7-e18ede788ffc","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:28.355Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b02013fd-7906-447c-bbd8-984035ef4bf9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011DCTba1h76hdq6aRU4hPLy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"py-spy windows install command record svg flamegraph PID\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - benfred/py-spy: Sampling profiler for Python programs · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/benfred/py-spy\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/py-spy/0.1.3/\"},{\"title\":\"Profiling Python code with py-spy - wrighters.io\",\"url\":\"https://www.wrighters.io/profiling-python-code-with-py-spy/\"},{\"title\":\"Py-spy: A Powerful and User-Friendly Python Performance Profiling Tool | by Meng Li | Top Python Libraries | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/top-python-libraries/py-spy-a-powerful-and-user-friendly-python-performance-profiling-tool-95f81636b8b1\"},{\"title\":\"Python Performance Profiling Using PySpy — Squirro Documentation\",\"url\":\"https://docs.squirro.com/en/latest/technical/admin/troubleshooting/python-profiling-pyspy.html\"},{\"title\":\"Spying on Python with py-spy\",\"url\":\"https://codilime.com/blog/spying-on-python-with-py-spy/\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy 0.1.4 - Docs.rs\",\"url\":\"https://docs.rs/crate/py-spy/0.1.4\"},{\"title\":\"Installation and Usage | benfred/py-spy | DeepWiki\",\"url\":\"https://deepwiki.com/benfred/py-spy/1.1-installation-and-usage\"},{\"title\":\"py-spy - Sampling Profiler for Python Code by Sunny Solanki\",\"url\":\"https://coderzcolumn.com/tutorials/python/py-spy-sampling-profiler-for-python-code\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - benfred/py-spy: Sampling profiler for Python programs\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/benfred/py-spy/tree/master\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's the information about py-spy installation and usage on Windows:\n\n## Installation\n\nTo install py-spy, use pip:\n```\npip install py-spy\n```\n\n## Record Command with Flamegraph\n\nTo generate a flame graph of your python process, you can use: `py-spy record -o profile.svg --pid 12345`\n\nAlternatively, you can use: `py-spy record -o profile.svg -- python myprogram.py`\n\n## Key Details for Windows\n\nOn Windows, py-spy attempts to get thread activity information by looking if the current SysCall is known to be idle, making it compatible with Windows systems.\n\n## Command Structure\n\npy-spy works from the command line and takes either the PID of the program you want to sample from or the command line of the python program you want to run.\n\nThe `record` command can generate flame graphs in SVG format, and you can change the file format to generate speedscope profiles or raw data with the `--format` parameter.\n\n## Additional Options\n\nSee `py-spy record --help` for information on other options including changing the sampling rate, filtering to only include threads that hold the GIL, profiling native C extensions, showing thread-ids, profiling subprocesses and more.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"ff27a6b7-2c48-414e-a21b-8e87578aa64b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:31.979Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b02013fd-7906-447c-bbd8-984035ef4bf9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6c371fc7-9e31-4e7b-80f2-3de7f4720092","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018hYAhNkxPqANif5jrvKP7p","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT log_subsolver_statistics num_search_workers benchmark tuning\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver couldn't prove the solution is optimal if num_workers < 5 but otherwise it did instantly. · Issue #3662 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3662\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Custom combination of subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/pQ4B7QD6ebw\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"cpsat-logutils · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cpsat-logutils/\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver takes way too long to shut down or does not stop at all despite given time limit · Issue #4882 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4882\"},{\"title\":\"Speeding up the time to find a first solution\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/AI6oGqyhx5Q\"},{\"title\":\"Performance regression in `main` after an optimal solution already found · Issue #4268 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4268\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-logutils: Utils for parsing the logs of OR-Tools' CP-SAT solver.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-logutils\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CP-SAT's `log_subsolver_statistics`, `num_search_workers`, and benchmark tuning:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**num_search_workers Parameter:**\nBy default, CP-SAT leverages all available cores (including hyperthreading), and you can control the parallelization of CP-SAT by setting the number of search workers.\n\nWith a single worker, only the default subsolver is used. With two workers or more, CP-SAT starts using incomplete subsolvers (i.e., heuristics such as LNS). With five workers, CP-SAT will also have a first solution subsolver. With 32 workers, all 15 full problem subsolvers are used. For more than 32 workers, primarily the number of first solution subsolvers is increased.\n\n**Tuning Recommendations:**\nThe CP-SAT solver is tuned for 8 and 16 workers, mostly for 16 workers lately.\n\nFine-tuning such a solver is not a simple task, and often you do more harm than good by tinkering around. However, decreasing the number of search workers can actually improve the runtime for some problems. This indicates that at least selecting the right subsolvers that are best fitted for your problem can be worth a shot.\n\n**log_subsolver_statistics:**\nThis parameter can be enabled to get detailed logging about subsolver activity. Libraries like cpsat-logutils extract key information from CP-SAT logs (solutions, bounds, presolve stats, subsolver activity, search progress, conflicts, etc.) and expose them in structured Python objects.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"53f7535b-61ec-45c7-8bce-99f744fc0e7e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:34.813Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6c371fc7-9e31-4e7b-80f2-3de7f4720092","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"18485255-a3ad-4cba-a3a7-e18ede788ffc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"263c4537-7eaf-4141-8243-2bd92971b2f2","agentId":"a03cda5a8b71604d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XR2oDKx1A8f1FYNKC87Xv2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"superluminal\" OR \"tracy\" profiler windows C++ sampling 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Superluminal\",\"url\":\"https://superluminal.eu/\"},{\"title\":\"Profiling for C++ - Superluminal\",\"url\":\"https://superluminal.eu/cplusplus/\"},{\"title\":\"Interpreting Superluminal Profiler Samples | Sebastian Schöner\",\"url\":\"https://blog.s-schoener.com/2025-02-13-superluminal-compute-memory/\"},{\"title\":\"Unity Profiling using Superluminal - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/attiliohimeki/unity-profiling-using-superluminal-2oc8\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - aclysma/profiling: Provides a very thin abstraction over instrumented profiling crates like puffin, optick, tracy, and superluminal-perf. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/aclysma/profiling\"},{\"title\":\"CPU Profiling Support for Open 3D Engine (O3DE) - Open 3D Engine\",\"url\":\"https://www.docs.o3de.org/docs/user-guide/profiling/cpu_profiling/\"},{\"title\":\"Tracy: A hybrid frame and sampling profiler for games and other applications | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=34559993\"},{\"title\":\"Features - Superluminal\",\"url\":\"https://superluminal.eu/features/\"},{\"title\":\"Tracy: A real time, nanosecond resolution frame profiler | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41632719\"},{\"title\":\"Superluminal Performance 1.0.7115.1289 - FileCR\",\"url\":\"https://filecr.com/windows/superluminal-performance/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Superluminal and Tracy profilers for Windows C++ sampling:\n\n## Superluminal\n\nSuperluminal is the new industry-standard profiler for C/C++ and Rust on Windows, Xbox One and PlayStation 4. Key features include:\n\n- **Sampling Capability**: Superluminal features unprecedented precision through its high frequency sampling engine (8KHz+).\n- **Visual UI**: Superluminal is the only sampling profiler that displays the profiling data in a visual UI, with sampling data displayed on a per-thread timeline, which allows you to see exactly what function is being called when, in what order, and what other functions are being called around it.\n- **C++ Support**: It has full support for C++ symbol resolving and name demangling, and both MSVC and Clang are fully supported.\n- **No Code Modification**: High frequency sampling (8 – 40 kHz, depending on platform) allows you to hit the ground running without the need to make any code modifications, and sampling can start right from the start of the application.\n\n## Tracy\n\nTracy requires you to add macros to your codebase to log functions/scopes, so it's not an automatic sampling profiler like superluminal, verysleepy, VS profiler, or others. However, each of those macros has around 50 nanoseconds of overhead, so you can liberally use them in the millions.\n\nThe key distinction is that **Superluminal is a sampling-based profiler** that requires no code instrumentation, while **Tracy is an instrumentation-based profiler** requiring manual code annotations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"197cb8d9-1a86-40f5-aab9-69cb86a94da2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:09:35.389Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"18485255-a3ad-4cba-a3a7-e18ede788ffc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
